- time: '1:01:57'
  seconds: 3717
  topic: Public Comment
  desc: The first public comment session featured parents, teachers, and community
    members. Many speakers thanked the board for delaying reconfiguration but urged
    them to create a better process and provide support for the Kaler community. Others
    expressed frustration that the delay prolongs inequity and places an unfair burden
    on Kaler families.
  children:
  - seconds: 3873
    speaker: Sam Merrill
    text: As a parent and member of the disabled community, Merrill urged the board
      to consider the extra time and logistical challenges transitions pose for people
      with disabilities. He asked that this perspective be included in future planning
      for school transportation and scheduling.
  - seconds: 4026
    speaker: Sarah Gay
    text: Speaking as SPTA President, Gay expressed gratitude for the reconfiguration
      delay and shared teacher survey data indicating a lack of trust in the budget
      process. She stressed the urgent need to support the Kaler community, whose
      school is still closing, and ensure they know their placements as soon as possible.
  - seconds: 4218
    speaker: Aidan Renaghan
    text: A parent who advocated for the delay, Renaghan emphasized the need for the
      board to make a clear, data-driven case for future changes. He called for more
      targeted community engagement to gather input and more transparency about the
      trade-offs involved in any decision.
  - seconds: 4407
    speaker: Vladimir Korshin
    text: Korshin thanked the board for the delay and stated the focus should now
      be on the Kaler community. He questioned if it was still possible to keep Kaler
      open and suggested exploring a voter initiative or ballot measure to see if
      the city would fund it.
  - seconds: 4520
    speaker: Alex Strube
    text: Strube thanked the board for listening and asked them to articulate a broader
      vision for education in the district beyond just minimizing the harm of budget
      cuts. He urged the community to be involved in shaping what the district is
      trying to create for its children.
  - seconds: 4621
    speaker: Ralph Baxter
    text: A former board member, Baxter applauded the board for listening but expressed
      concern over recent turmoil, including a member's resignation and police presence
      at meetings. He recommended the board hold a facilitated workshop to set goals
      and work on a new, unified tone.
  - seconds: 4816
    speaker: Amy
    text: A Kaler parent, Amy described how the reconfiguration delay feels profoundly
      unfair to the Kaler community, which she described as one of the most vulnerable
      in the district. She stated that Kaler families are not afforded the luxury
      of more time and must bear the brunt of the disruption.
  - seconds: 4929
    speaker: Mindy Aloes
    text: A parent who served on the configuration committee, Aloes discouraged asking
      for more tax money and argued against the delay, stating the conversation is
      not new. She asserted that delaying reconfiguration is incredibly unfair to
      Kaler families and staff, who may now face two disruptive transitions in two
      years.
  - seconds: 5059
    speaker: Chloe Bartlett
    text: A third-grade teacher at Dyer, Bartlett described the stress and trauma
      staff have felt due to budget cuts and the loss of colleagues. She asked the
      board how they will ensure this process is not repeated for a third consecutive
      year.
