  children: []
- time: '2:28:00'
  seconds: 8880
  topic: Public Comment
  desc: The second public comment session included questions about board processes
    and further advocacy. Speakers questioned the origin of a budget motion, advocated
    for restoring cut middle school programs, and debated the merits of tax increases
    versus fiscal restraint.
  children:
  - seconds: 8943
    speaker: Sarah Gay
    text: Gay asked who wrote the budget motion on the agenda, whether the district
      would offer exit interviews to departing staff, and if subcontracting ed techs
      costs more than hiring them. She also repeated her question about whether the
      board's goal is an "excellent" or merely "adequate" education.
  - seconds: 9059
    speaker: Meredith Diamond
    text: Diamond criticized what she termed the district's negligent decision-making
      and financial mismanagement, pointing to the projected $1.1 million deficit.
      She argued that financial solvency is a key strategy for equity and urged the
      board to take accountability for the crisis.
  - seconds: 9225
    speaker: Charlie Fox
    text: Fox expressed concern over the board's decision to direct potential savings
      to contingency rather than restoring teaching positions. He argued this devalues
      teachers and programs, hurts the district's reputation, and will make it harder
      to retain top educators.
  - seconds: 9343
    speaker: Caroline Bowman
    text: Bowman advocated for restoring the middle school related arts positions,
      highlighting the specific, positive contributions of the teachers being cut.
      She argued these programs are a critical safety net and source of belonging
      for students during their most volatile developmental years.
  - seconds: 9542
    speaker: Ed Cobb
    text: A resident and taxpayer, Cobb spoke against continued tax increases, calculating
      that a 6% annual increase would result in a $10,000 average tax bill in less
      than a decade. He argued that at some point, there must be a balance between
      educational wants and what taxpayers can bear.
  - seconds: 9727
    speaker: Morgan Kerr
    text: A Kaler parent, Kerr expressed disappointment with the decision to delay
      reconfiguration, arguing it misses an opportunity for the community to unite.
      She stated that if any other school were closing, she would support reconfiguration
      because it is what's best for all kids, not just her own.
  - seconds: 9844
    speaker: Vladimir Korshin
    text: Korshin spoke in favor of tax increases, arguing they are necessary to keep
      up with inflation and pay teachers a living wage for quality services. He urged
      the board not to be afraid to put tax increases on the ballot and let the voters
      decide.
  - seconds: 9909
    speaker: Lauren Shapiro
    text: Shapiro thanked the board for the delay but stressed that Kaler deserves
      to be part of the process. She raised concerns about rising class sizes, the
      loss of diverse educators, and the underreporting of dangerous incidents in
      schools, asking for more comprehensive data to be made public.
- time: '2:48:04'
  seconds: 10084
  topic: Public Comment Q&A
  desc: The board and administration addressed questions from the second public comment.
